        "contenuto_esteso": "To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. 'Unable to cope': Malian army, Wagner 'overstretched' by Tuareg separatists and jihadist militants Africa Issued on: 27/04/2026 - 10:10 Modified: 27/04/2026 - 10:13 Oliver Farry is pleased to welcome Paul Melly, Consulting Fellow on the Africa Program at Chatham House. According to Melly, what we are witnessing is the convergence of multiple fractures: political, territorial, and military. The recent attacks illustrate a dramatic expansion of jihadist operational reach into the very heart of state power. While the Malian state might not be collapsing outright, its authority is stretched dangerously thin across Africa's 8th largest country. The withdrawal of int'l actors, combined with the limits of partnerships, has exposed structural vulnerabilities. Russian mercenaries in Mali: Limited gains, reports of abuse 26/04/2026 Africa Malian Defence Minister Sadio Camara killed by rebels 26/04/2026 Africa Gunfire and blasts rock Mali: Army claims situation is under control 26/04/2026 Africa Armed groups claim attacks across Mali 26/04/2026 Africa Attacks on Bamako a 'dramatic setback' for Malian government 25/04/2026 Africa FLA claims attacks on Malian capital 25/04/2026 Africa Coordinated attacks hit Bamako and Northern Mali 25/04/2026 Africa Gunfire rocks Bamako: 'Too soon to tell' if junta will fall 25/04/2026 Africa 'Dire humanitarian crisis': Hundreds of thousands displaced in Ethiopia's Tigray 25/04/2026 Africa"
